WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.140
GREG CASTLE: So when
we were thinking

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.550
about the ideas
for this talk, we

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.320
were kind of
looking at customers

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.000
who were trying to isolate
things within their cluster.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.650
And so a typical
scenario is you have

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.600
some sort of trusted
workload and some sort

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.070
of untrusted workload.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.710
The examples we use in the
talk are a payment system

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.840
is the trusted
side, and a company

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.480
that is doing web
hosting has a container

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.380
that's provided by a customer.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.050
And that's basically
untrusted code

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.660
from a security perspective,
and so people want

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.550
to separate these things out.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.980
So you could use a cluster
boundary, but a lot of people

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.920
are actually looking at
kind of putting the trusted

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.930
thing on trusted nodes
and the untrusted thing

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.260
on untrusted nodes, so
using a VM and a node,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.040
effectively, to separate
those two kinds of workloads.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.580
And that is a pretty comfortable
place for security people.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.227
They are used to
separating things with VMs,

00:00:51.227 --> 00:00:53.560
but in Kubernetes, it's
actually a little bit different.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.473
There's actually quite a lot
of connections between nodes

00:00:57.473 --> 00:00:58.890
and a lot of
privilege that's held

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.110
by nodes that makes
that isolation

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.630
just probably not as good as
a lot of people think it is.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.880
And so we really wanted to
highlight that in this talk

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.410
and give a practical
demonstration

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:12.480
of how to break that
isolation and talk

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.510
about some alternatives.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.210
Tim, do you want to talk
about node versus pod,

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.218
and what the
alternatives are there?

00:01:18.218 --> 00:01:19.260
TIM ALLCLAIR: Yeah, sure.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.400
So the node is
really complicated.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.390
There's a lot of stuff
going on, and the attacker--

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.280
an attacker who has
compromised the node

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.910
has access to the union of
all of those different things

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.330
that the node is using.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:32.910
They have a pretty
large toolkit,

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.300
even if you're running
the least privileged.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.882
And so we kind of compare
what the attacker has access

00:01:38.882 --> 00:01:40.590
to in the node with
what they have access

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.770
to in the pod, which you
can scope down much more,

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.540
and this slide summarizes
the differences there.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.230
And then we wrap up by looking
at what you can actually

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.360
do to defend, in addition to--

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.190
or instead of node isolation.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.790
Before the attacker can
get to actually leveraging

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.740
all of those privileges
at the node level,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.960
they first have to go
through your application,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.030
getting remote code execution.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.780
They then need to break
out of the container,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.900
and only then can they get
access to the whole node.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.000
So first, we recommend
hardening the application.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.940
Then we look at different ways
of hardening the container,

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.850
and we talk about sandboxes,
like GKE sandboxes with gVisor

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.910
as a way of hardening that
hard boundary a lot more.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.730
And then we see
that, at that point,

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.970
node isolation
isn't as critical.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.000
So we finish off by
talking about how

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.040
nodes are really
complicated and shouldn't

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.220
be your only measure of
defense, and instead, we

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.450
recommend using container
isolation and pod sandboxing

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.420
as a much more secure
form of isolation.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.170
GREG CASTLE: So I hope
you enjoy the talk.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.195
And we'll be hanging
out at the Google booth,

00:02:48.195 --> 00:02:49.820
so please come by
and talk to us if you

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.830
want to talk about security and
GKE and isolation or anything

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:53.820
else.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.220
Thanks.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

